# LifeOps configuration. Copy to .env and edit.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# There is no secret here. Nothing captured leaves your network and no third party is
# involved, so there is no account to make, no API key to rotate, and nothing in this file
# that would hurt you if someone read it. LLM_API_KEY exists only for the operator who has
# put their own model server behind a proxy that wants one.
#
# Only the first two matter. Everything below them has a working default.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Required
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Your OpenAI-compatible model endpoint, including the API path. Ollama serves it on
# :11434/v1 — `http://<host>:11434`, without the /v1, is refused at startup.
#
# host.docker.internal is the machine running Docker, as seen from inside a container.
# Use it when the model server runs on this same host; use the LAN address (or a Tailscale
# name) when it runs on the box with the GPU. Never `localhost` — inside a container that
# is the container, and LifeOps will refuse to start rather than pretend otherwise.
#
# It must be on your own network: loopback, a private/link-local/CGNAT address, or a name
# only a local resolver can answer. A hosted provider is rejected, by design.
LLM_BASE_URL=http://host.docker.internal:11434/v1

# The model as your endpoint names it — exactly what `ollama list` prints. Startup checks
# the endpoint actually serves it and tells you what it does serve if not.
LLM_MODEL=qwen3.5:2b-q4_K_M

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional — sensible defaults, listed so you know they exist
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The zone you live in. Every calendar date the system derives is computed in it, including
# the day it tells the model a note was written on, so a note dumped near midnight resolves
# "tomorrow" the way you meant it. Defaults to the container's zone, which is UTC.
LIFEOPS_TIMEZONE=America/Toronto

# Only if your endpoint sits behind a proxy that wants a credential. Ollama ignores it.
LLM_API_KEY=

# How hard the model reasons, passed straight through. Unset means the endpoint's own
# default, which is reasoning on: better extraction, and minutes per dump rather than
# seconds. `none` is the fast, worse option. See the README before changing it.
# LLM_REASONING_EFFORT=none

# How long one extraction may take before it is abandoned and retried. A stall detector,
# not a latency budget — reasoning-on extraction genuinely runs to about six minutes.
# LLM_TIMEOUT_MS=600000

# How long the worker sleeps when there is nothing to extract.
# WORKER_POLL_MS=2000

# Attempts before a dump is marked terminally failed.
# EXTRACTION_MAX_ATTEMPTS=3

# Where the SQLite file lives. docker-compose.yml pins this to /data/lifeops.db on the one
# mounted volume and ignores what is set here — moving state off that volume would break
# "a backup is a copy of one volume". Set it only when running from source.
# LIFEOPS_DB_PATH=./data/lifeops.db

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Compose only
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The host port the capture box is published on. LifeOps ships no auth and no TLS: bind it
# to a LAN or VPN address, or put a reverse proxy in front. See docs/DEPLOY.md.
# LIFEOPS_PORT=3000

# Which published image to run. `latest` is whatever last landed on main; pin to sha-<commit>
# if you would rather upgrade deliberately.
# LIFEOPS_IMAGE_TAG=latest
